text:
Maria T.: FYI escalation from AcmeApp. They report our JSON-mode streaming responses are frequently truncated mid-object and their parser fails with "Unexpected end of input". Happening with our Node SDK and direct curl.

Maria T.: Logs from customer (they redacted keys):
```
Request: POST /v1/generate/stream
Headers: Authorization: Bearer REDACTED, Accept: text/event-stream
Body: { mode: "json_schema", schema: {...}, prompt: "generate order" }

Partial stream received (last chunk):
data: {"id":"tok_123","output": {"orders": [ {"id": "o_1","items": [ {"sku": "A1","qty": 2} ], "total": 42.5 }, {"id": "o_2","items": [ {"sku": "B2","qty": 1}
// stream ends abruptly here (no closing brackets)
```

Maria T.: They see this ~1.2% of requests when streaming enabled. Non-streaming (full response) always valid. Customer is very sensitive because they pipe directly into a strict JSON consumer.

Maria T.: @ryank @sashal can you triage? Customer included their repro steps. Acme can share a recorded tcpdump if needed.

Ryan K.: Thanks, looking. Quick q: are they using the `stream=true` SSE-style endpoint or our chunked transfer stream? Could be different.

Maria T.: They said SSE (`text/event-stream`) and also tried with our Node SDK streaming helper which uses fetch + readable stream.

Sasha L.: On the surface this reads like early connection close on client side or our runtime yielding an early partial chunk. Two branches: 1) client disconnects, we stop streaming mid-JSON; 2) runtime trims/flushes incorrectly on model early stop (stop reason mismatch).

Ryan K.: Can you paste the SDK streaming read loop they used? Sometimes people parse chunk-by-chunk and assume each chunk is a full JSON doc.

Maria T.: Customer provided this snippet (Node):
```
const res = await fetch(url, { headers, method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
const reader = res.body.getReader();
let buf = '';
while (true) {
  const { done, value } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  buf += new TextDecoder().decode(value);
  // naive: try JSON.parse when we see "}"
  if (buf.includes('}')) {
    try {
      const obj = JSON.parse(buf);
      handle(obj);
      buf = '';
    } catch (e) {
      // ignore until complete
    }
  }
}
```

Maria T.: They tried a more robust stream parser too and still got invalid final payloads (parser fails because final closing braces never arrive).

Sasha L.: Ok good to have that. @ryank can you try to reproduce with a local small server that simulates the pattern? Also check our connection close handling in the streaming codepath.

Ryan K.: Reproducing now with a script that requests `stream=true` and then forcibly close socket from my end to see how runtime reacts. Will paste logs.

(20m later)

Ryan K.: Repro done. If client closes socket ungracefully we currently treat it as a normal end-of-stream and do not append the final buffered token flush under some kernel scheduling races. I can reproduce by sending a SYN-RST mid-stream.

Ryan K.: Repro steps (linux netcat + curl):
```
# start simple SSE client
curl -N -H "Accept: text/event-stream" -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate/stream -d '{"mode":"json_schema","prompt":"..."}' &
# identify pid and send kill -9 to simulate abrupt close
kill -9 <pid>
```
Result: our runtime sometimes logs `client disconnected` and the worker spins down before flushing final KV cache -> final chunk lost.

Sasha L.: That points to a runtime flush race. Two immediate mitigations: 1) server side ensure flush of last bytes synchronously before worker exit; 2) add a small finalizing sentinel event in protocol (e.g., `data: __END_JSON__`) that consumers can wait for.

Nina P.: Customer request: they want a workaround now (can't disable streaming long-term). Any immediate suggestion to recommend?

Ryan K.: Short-term: suggest they use our non-streaming mode for critical operations OR implement a tolerant parser that buffers until both `\n` and `}` appear and then try to repair (but that's fragile). Better: wait for final SSE `event: done` (we already sometimes send `event: done` on clean completion). If they don't see `event: done`, treat as partial and retry the request.

Maria T.: I replied to customer with: 1) ask them to retry and check for `event: done` before parsing; 2) we are investigating root cause and will patch runtime; 3) offer to route their traffic to dedicated infra in the meantime for a short window.

Ryan K.: I'll open a runtime issue and a short patch to ensure a synchronous flush on worker shutdown. Will add an opt-in config to enable `graceful_stream_flush=true` default false for canary.

Ryan K.: Draft PR posted: https://git.internal.redwood/r/redwood/runtime/pulls/842 - includes: ensure emitter flushes pending bytes on StopReason/Error, add heartbeat that forces chunk flush every 50ms.

deploy-bot: Starting canary deploy of runtime@patch/flush-842 to canary cluster (canary-1)

(1h later)

deploy-bot: Canary deploy succeeded on canary-1. Monitoring for 30m.

Sasha L.: Monitoring shows client disconnect-related truncation rate dropped from 1.2% -> 0.08% on canary. Good signal. We should roll to prod after 6h stable.

Nina P.: Customer updated: they routed some traffic to our canary tenant and saw no truncations over 2k requests. They asked for ETA to prod.

Ryan K.: Planning prod rollout in 6h pending no regressions. Will include changelog and note: streaming final flush fix + optional sentinel header `x-stream-final: true`.

Maria T.: Customer replied with thanks and asked if we can add a server-side indicator for incomplete streams we can return on request logs so they can auto-retry. Can we add an analytics flag for marked partial-streams?

Sasha L.: Yes we can add `stream.incomplete=true` attribute to request logs when we detect client disconnect or worker shutdown before final flush. I'll add to SLO logs and surface in Console under per-request traces.

Ryan K.: Prod rollout started. deploy-bot: Rolling out runtime@patch/flush-842 to prod cluster progressive.

(2h later)

deploy-bot: Rollout complete. No elevated error rates.

Maria T.: Customer confirmed they see 0 truncations in their next 5k requests. Closing for now but leaving notes for next-week retrospective to discuss protocol sentinel and SDK hints.

Sasha L.: Action items: (1) merge PR and add unit tests for flush on StopReason (ryan) (2) add `stream.incomplete` request log flag + Console surface (sasha) (3) update Node/py SDK docs with robust streaming parser examples and `event: done` guidance (nina) (4) consider protocol sentinel RFC (team).

Maria T.: Thanks all. I logged the thread in customer tickets and informed AcmeApp of the timeline. :thumbsup:
